  "heat": 1,
  "text": "Everybody thinks Jesus told Mary off in this story. He said Martha's name. Twice. Jesus comes into a village. Martha receives him into her house. Her house. She is the host. This was her call. Her sister Mary sits down at his feet and just listens. And Martha is what the text calls cumbered about much serving. Cumbered. Dragged in every direction. Hosting is real work and she is doing all of it, alone, while her sister sits on the floor. So Martha does what a lot of us do. She does not go to Mary. She goes over her head. \"Lord, dost thou not care that my sister hath left me to serve alone? Bid her therefore that she help me.\" She is not asking. She is telling him to sort out her sister. And here is where everybody expects the scolding. He says, \"Martha, Martha.\" Her name. Twice. That is not how you open a telling off. That is how you talk to someone you love who is drowning. \"Thou art careful and troubled about many things. But one thing is needful, and Mary hath chosen that good part, which shall not be taken away from her.\" Now read what he did not say. He never tells Mary to get up. He never says the serving was wrong. He tells Martha she is allowed to sit down too.",
